{{Church Governance and Structure}}

One of Calvin's most lasting influences on the Presbyterian Church is its distinctive form of governance. Calvin advocated for a church structure that was both representative and decentralized, emphasizing the role of elders (presbyters) in church leadership. This system, known as presbyterian polity, differs significantly from hierarchical models, promoting shared leadership and accountability. By implementing a system of elected elders and ministers who govern collectively, Calvin ensured that church authority was distributed among its members, fostering a sense of community and shared responsibility that persists in Presbyterian congregations today.

{{Emphasis on Education and Community}}

Calvin's insistence on education and community engagement has deeply influenced the Presbyterian Church's values. He believed that an educated laity was essential for the flourishing of the church and society. Consequently, his advocacy for widespread literacy and education led to the establishment of schools and universities that were accessible to a broader population. This focus on learning and community service became a hallmark of Presbyterian identity, encouraging members to engage thoughtfully with their faith and the world around them. The emphasis on education not only empowered individuals but also strengthened the community as a whole, fostering a culture of informed and active participation in both church and civic life.

