To set up a reverse proxy with Nginx, you can use the following configuration example. This configuration includes placeholders for different server names or IP addresses, allowing you to direct traffic to multiple backend servers. Start by defining an upstream block that groups the backend servers. Here's a basic setup:

```nginx
http {
    upstream backend_servers {
        server placeholder_server1;
        server placeholder_server2;
    }

    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name example.com;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://backend_servers;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        }
    }
}
```

In this configuration, `placeholder_server1` and `placeholder_server2` are placeholders where you can insert the actual IP addresses or domain names of your backend servers. The `server` block listens on port 80 for incoming HTTP requests and forwards these requests to the `backend_servers` group defined earlier. The `proxy_set_header` directives ensure that the necessary headers are forwarded to the backend servers, preserving the original request information. Customize the `server_name` directive with your domain name and replace the placeholders with your actual server details to complete the setup.

